No—despite being geometrically above the horizon, the Moon would not realistically be visible to your unaided eye.

At that moment it is:

- only **0.09% illuminated**, an extraordinarily thin crescent;
- just **3.445° from the Sun**, deep in the Sun’s bright glare;
- only about **10.5° above the horizon**, where atmospheric extinction and scattering are substantial;
- in **full daylight**, with the Sun already **13.7° high**.

This is essentially a near-new Moon. Even under exceptionally transparent skies, unaided-eye crescent sightings generally require a substantially greater angular separation from the Sun and a thicker illuminated fraction. Here the crescent’s low contrast would be overwhelmed by the bright sky.

So the practical answer is **no**. Also, because it is so close to the Sun, do not try to locate it with binoculars or a telescope unless the Sun is securely blocked and appropriate solar-safety procedures are used.

